WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:12.450
&gt;&gt; Welcome Manuel. It's
great having you here.

00:00:12.451 --> 00:00:13.740
I hope you enjoyed yourself.

00:00:13.741 --> 00:00:16.245
&gt;&gt; Thank you, Eric.
It has been great.

00:00:16.246 --> 00:00:19.590
First of all, it's a wonderful
playground, and secondly,

00:00:19.591 --> 00:00:22.755
you have these very
interesting people,

00:00:22.756 --> 00:00:24.540
one after the other.

00:00:24.541 --> 00:00:26.670
&gt;&gt; It's a kind of place one

00:00:26.671 --> 00:00:29.800
can stay for like 25
years and not get tired.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:35.190
Yes, so it's interesting because

00:00:35.191 --> 00:00:38.835
I've followed the work that
you are well known for,

00:00:38.836 --> 00:00:43.065
for which you received
the Turing Award a few years back.

00:00:43.066 --> 00:00:48.515
I personally didn't know
about your deep interest in

00:00:48.516 --> 00:00:53.975
brains and minds before some little
recent discussion we had, so-

00:00:53.976 --> 00:00:57.890
&gt;&gt; I think that the rest
of it was side.

00:00:57.891 --> 00:00:59.780
I got pushed in the wrong direction.

00:00:59.781 --> 00:01:02.940
I'm finally doing what
I really want to do.

00:01:02.941 --> 00:01:04.500
&gt;&gt; Tell me a bit more
about the background.

00:01:04.501 --> 00:01:07.250
You mentioned this really cool story
during your talk yesterday.

00:01:07.251 --> 00:01:08.420
Or I say fun story.

00:01:08.421 --> 00:01:09.710
&gt;&gt; How I got into this?

00:01:09.711 --> 00:01:13.955
&gt;&gt; Yeah, your father
and his request to you.

00:01:13.956 --> 00:01:18.620
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, the beginning
was when I was a young kid,

00:01:18.621 --> 00:01:21.350
I learned English in the Bronx when

00:01:21.351 --> 00:01:24.365
I was about to go
into the first grade.

00:01:24.366 --> 00:01:26.000
&gt;&gt; What was your first language?

00:01:26.001 --> 00:01:28.805
&gt;&gt; My first language was
German for four years.

00:01:28.806 --> 00:01:31.130
Then my parents came to this country.

00:01:31.131 --> 00:01:34.355
Nobody liked to hear German because
they were at war with Germany.

00:01:34.356 --> 00:01:36.245
So they switched to Spanish.

00:01:36.246 --> 00:01:38.330
I always spoke a language that nobody

00:01:38.331 --> 00:01:44.135
understood until I went
into grade school.

00:01:44.136 --> 00:01:47.630
I remember, I knew I
had to learn English,

00:01:47.631 --> 00:01:49.040
I was going to go to first grade.

00:01:49.041 --> 00:01:53.015
I saw two women in the corner
speaking to each other

00:01:53.016 --> 00:01:55.880
and I stood there with

00:01:55.881 --> 00:01:58.750
my back to them so they
wouldn't know I'm spying.

00:01:58.751 --> 00:02:02.465
I remember listening to them and
telling myself exactly this,

00:02:02.466 --> 00:02:05.090
"I will never learn this language,

00:02:05.091 --> 00:02:08.150
they speak much too fast for me."

00:02:08.151 --> 00:02:10.310
The interesting thing about this is

00:02:10.311 --> 00:02:12.110
I'm remembering that in English,

00:02:12.111 --> 00:02:14.660
and it couldn't have been English.

00:02:14.661 --> 00:02:17.615
&gt;&gt; That's funny, some sort of
internal translation over the years.

00:02:17.616 --> 00:02:18.980
&gt;&gt; Since I didn't speak English,

00:02:18.981 --> 00:02:21.335
I didn't do that well in school,

00:02:21.336 --> 00:02:24.950
and by the time I got to the
second grade, the teacher,

00:02:24.951 --> 00:02:30.610
I was pretty dumb and
the teacher said to my mother,

00:02:30.611 --> 00:02:33.980
parents-teachers
conference, "You know,

00:02:33.981 --> 00:02:35.300
he may be able to get through

00:02:35.301 --> 00:02:38.805
high school but he'll
never get to college."

00:02:38.806 --> 00:02:41.540
So this bothered me because I-

00:02:41.541 --> 00:02:43.390
&gt;&gt; So that was actually
shared with you?

00:02:43.391 --> 00:02:44.650
&gt;&gt; That was shared with me.

00:02:44.651 --> 00:02:44.970
&gt;&gt; I see.

00:02:44.971 --> 00:02:45.625
&gt;&gt; Third or fourth grade.

00:02:45.626 --> 00:02:48.295
&gt;&gt; You knew your limitations, yeah.

00:02:48.296 --> 00:02:51.550
&gt;&gt; Yeah. It was before
the fourth grade.

00:02:51.551 --> 00:02:54.655
So yeah, it was second grade.

00:02:54.656 --> 00:03:00.050
So the teacher advised my mother
to start speaking English to me,

00:03:00.051 --> 00:03:03.785
and my mother told her,

00:03:03.786 --> 00:03:05.855
"I don't speak English."

00:03:05.856 --> 00:03:10.260
Teacher responded, "Well, what do
you think you're talking to me?"

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:15.305
So when my mother switched,
I learned English.

00:03:15.306 --> 00:03:20.690
I remember that when
I switch to Spanish,

00:03:20.691 --> 00:03:22.370
for four-five year to German.

00:03:22.371 --> 00:03:23.710
&gt;&gt; Now why Spanish?

00:03:23.711 --> 00:03:25.440
&gt;&gt; I was born in Venezuela.

00:03:25.441 --> 00:03:26.400
&gt;&gt; Ah, that's right. Okay.

00:03:26.401 --> 00:03:27.450
&gt;&gt; Where they all speak Spanish.

00:03:27.451 --> 00:03:29.330
So my first language is German,

00:03:29.331 --> 00:03:31.895
when they speak Spanish
I didn't understand.

00:03:31.896 --> 00:03:35.885
I remember when my mother
switched to Spanish in the Bronx,

00:03:35.886 --> 00:03:39.245
that one month later
after she had switched,

00:03:39.246 --> 00:03:41.240
she discovered, she realized to

00:03:41.241 --> 00:03:44.150
her shock that I had
forgotten my German.

00:03:44.151 --> 00:03:47.555
I could see her at the
window saying, "Window.

00:03:47.556 --> 00:03:48.695
How do you say window?

00:03:48.696 --> 00:03:52.980
Fenster." Because I didn't know that.

00:03:52.981 --> 00:03:56.360
So I pride myself in being
able to learn languages

00:03:56.361 --> 00:03:59.015
but I'm even better
at forgetting them.

00:03:59.016 --> 00:04:01.205
&gt;&gt; That's great. It keeps
your mind flexible.

00:04:01.206 --> 00:04:05.360
So knowing that you might finish
high school and then go off

00:04:05.361 --> 00:04:07.400
to do something that was

00:04:07.401 --> 00:04:10.010
probably non-intellectual
for the rest of your life.

00:04:10.011 --> 00:04:12.035
How did you take to this?

00:04:12.036 --> 00:04:14.090
The prognosis from the teacher

00:04:14.091 --> 00:04:16.310
and being passed up by your parents.

00:04:16.311 --> 00:04:19.875
&gt;&gt; Oh yeah. So I really wanted
to be able to think better.

00:04:19.876 --> 00:04:23.790
And I was having trouble with memory.

00:04:23.791 --> 00:04:25.425
Couldn't remember stuff.

00:04:25.426 --> 00:04:27.975
So I asked my father,

00:04:27.976 --> 00:04:29.775
"How can I think better?

00:04:29.776 --> 00:04:31.665
How can I memorize?"

00:04:31.666 --> 00:04:35.235
He just said, "Well, you just do it."

00:04:35.236 --> 00:04:38.320
Which didn't help me at all.

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:43.980
Then after I pestered him
enough, he suggested,

00:04:43.981 --> 00:04:47.595
"You know, if you understood
how the brain works,

00:04:47.596 --> 00:04:50.490
that would help you
to become smarter."

00:04:50.491 --> 00:04:55.645
I consider that the most wonderful
advice that one can give a kid.

00:04:55.646 --> 00:04:57.990
A kid in the first,

00:04:57.991 --> 00:05:02.690
second grade is just right
to get a focus for his life.

00:05:02.691 --> 00:05:04.790
That was just wonderful for me.

00:05:04.791 --> 00:05:06.320
&gt;&gt; Yes that's interesting
that we never really

00:05:06.321 --> 00:05:09.710
think that we don't

00:05:09.711 --> 00:05:12.680
have an owner's manual on
this incredible machine,

00:05:12.681 --> 00:05:17.765
and we're expected to
just somehow fly by wire,

00:05:17.766 --> 00:05:19.580
by learning as we go.

00:05:19.581 --> 00:05:21.650
When there's probably a lot we can do

00:05:21.651 --> 00:05:23.995
with reflection,
introspection, guidance,

00:05:23.996 --> 00:05:26.265
understanding how to learn better,

00:05:26.266 --> 00:05:29.630
how to challenge
ourselves intellectually.

00:05:29.631 --> 00:05:34.430
Why a math problem that seems
really hard shouldn't be a blocker,

00:05:34.431 --> 00:05:36.080
it should be a question
as to why I can't

00:05:36.081 --> 00:05:38.945
understand it, and to tackle,
and to think about it.

00:05:38.946 --> 00:05:41.090
&gt;&gt; Absolutely. Yeah.

00:05:41.091 --> 00:05:44.135
&gt;&gt; Now, do you think that
question from your father

00:05:44.136 --> 00:05:48.680
framed the curiosity you
have about minds and brains

00:05:48.681 --> 00:05:51.475
or were you already getting
curious about it on your own?

00:05:51.476 --> 00:05:53.850
&gt;&gt; Well, that was the beginning.

00:05:53.851 --> 00:05:58.740
But certainly, I'm lucky
that we moved a lot.

00:05:58.741 --> 00:06:01.155
So, I have my memories placed-

00:06:01.156 --> 00:06:02.655
&gt;&gt; Your living milestones.

00:06:02.656 --> 00:06:06.900
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So between
the third and fifth grade,

00:06:06.901 --> 00:06:09.690
we had moved out of the Bronx,

00:06:09.691 --> 00:06:12.480
I was in Westchester
County in New York.

00:06:12.481 --> 00:06:15.620
So I can remember I would go to

00:06:15.621 --> 00:06:19.595
the library and take
out books on the brain,

00:06:19.596 --> 00:06:21.500
and I was a little

00:06:21.501 --> 00:06:25.835
embarrassed because other kids
were taking out Black Beauty.

00:06:25.836 --> 00:06:28.940
&gt;&gt;You're trying to find
that owners manual sometimes-

00:06:28.941 --> 00:06:30.950
&gt;&gt; I'm trying to find
the owners manual. I would

00:06:30.951 --> 00:06:32.690
open the book to the back thing so

00:06:32.691 --> 00:06:34.700
that librarian could stamp it

00:06:34.701 --> 00:06:37.225
so she wouldn't see what
it was I was taking out.

00:06:37.226 --> 00:06:42.265
&gt;&gt; So, when you went
to MIT to do double E,

00:06:42.266 --> 00:06:46.610
the idea of you actually
finding your way into the lab,

00:06:46.611 --> 00:06:48.305
which to me is mind-blowing,

00:06:48.306 --> 00:06:50.000
of McCulloch and Pitts,

00:06:50.001 --> 00:06:52.370
because I read that paper
years ago I said, "Oh my god,

00:06:52.371 --> 00:06:53.780
these people are
the first people to write

00:06:53.781 --> 00:06:56.510
down and abstract what a neuron is.

00:06:56.511 --> 00:06:58.940
It's a mathematical unit
and then build circuits.

00:06:58.941 --> 00:07:01.740
Formal neuron and you actually
work with these people.

00:07:01.741 --> 00:07:03.405
&gt;&gt; Wonderful people.

00:07:03.406 --> 00:07:05.220
These were wonderful.

00:07:05.221 --> 00:07:09.950
Can't tell you, Marvin Minsky
was my thesis adviser,

00:07:09.951 --> 00:07:12.530
but Warren McCulloch
was really my mentor.

00:07:12.531 --> 00:07:13.715
&gt;&gt; That's fabulous.

00:07:13.716 --> 00:07:19.505
&gt;&gt; Walter Pitts, his sidekick
who really taught me a lot.

00:07:19.506 --> 00:07:22.735
Walter, for example, told me that,

00:07:22.736 --> 00:07:25.920
"Anything you want to know
you can get from books."

00:07:25.921 --> 00:07:28.740
Now, it's an overstatement

00:07:28.741 --> 00:07:29.840
because there are
things that we want to

00:07:29.841 --> 00:07:31.400
know that you cannot get from books,

00:07:31.401 --> 00:07:34.145
and that's the sort of thing
we do research for.

00:07:34.146 --> 00:07:37.685
But the truth is, there's
a lot you can get from books.

00:07:37.686 --> 00:07:40.250
Walter would go to a library
and take out books,

00:07:40.251 --> 00:07:41.390
and every so often,

00:07:41.391 --> 00:07:43.400
the librarian would tell him,

00:07:43.401 --> 00:07:46.765
"You got to bring those books back
so you can get a wheelbarrow."

00:07:46.766 --> 00:07:49.690
Literally, a wheelbarrow to be
able to bring the books back.

00:07:49.691 --> 00:07:52.520
&gt;&gt; Sounds like the overdue fines
would be quite extensive for him.

00:07:52.521 --> 00:07:57.150
You need a wheelbarrow
full of dollars and coins.

00:07:58.170 --> 00:08:01.720
Describe, I mean, people listening
to this maybe don't have

00:08:01.721 --> 00:08:05.980
the same admiration for
McCulloch and Pitts that I have,

00:08:05.981 --> 00:08:09.880
since being quite young and reading
about brains and minds myself

00:08:09.881 --> 00:08:13.585
but what they did with
their in paper 1943.

00:08:13.586 --> 00:08:17.710
&gt;&gt; Yeah. This wonderful paper that
introduced the formal neuron,

00:08:17.711 --> 00:08:21.010
and the formal neuron
with its inputs and they

00:08:21.011 --> 00:08:24.040
basically proved that you
have to have inhibition,

00:08:24.041 --> 00:08:27.400
not just with excitation alone,

00:08:27.401 --> 00:08:30.970
you could not have, they were doing,

00:08:30.971 --> 00:08:32.680
the theorem was that you could use

00:08:32.681 --> 00:08:36.385
these formal neurons to
construct a Turing machine.

00:08:36.386 --> 00:08:39.085
Nowadays, we know you can do that,

00:08:39.086 --> 00:08:40.345
but at that time,

00:08:40.346 --> 00:08:42.880
this was the paper that showed it.

00:08:42.881 --> 00:08:46.180
In the process of showing this,

00:08:46.181 --> 00:08:49.090
they saw that you
only can do very much

00:08:49.091 --> 00:08:52.855
unless these formal neurons get
both excited and inhibited.

00:08:52.856 --> 00:08:55.825
So they told the other
neurophysiologists

00:08:55.826 --> 00:08:58.630
there is inhibition in the brain,

00:08:58.631 --> 00:09:02.725
and the neurophysiologists
at the time said, "No.

00:09:02.726 --> 00:09:04.600
We've never seen them",

00:09:04.601 --> 00:09:08.665
and they said, "It's
got to be there".

00:09:08.666 --> 00:09:09.970
&gt;&gt; Now we know it's not only there

00:09:09.971 --> 00:09:11.815
but it's a major major player.

00:09:11.816 --> 00:09:14.905
&gt;&gt; Well, for sure it's
a major player. Yes.

00:09:14.906 --> 00:09:17.350
&gt;&gt; Very interesting and so-

00:09:17.351 --> 00:09:19.420
&gt;&gt; They founded after that,

00:09:19.421 --> 00:09:21.955
he insisted that the lesson-.

00:09:21.956 --> 00:09:24.190
&gt;&gt; You say they depended on during

00:09:24.191 --> 00:09:26.830
the- under the watch of McCulloch
and Pitts that we had timeframe?

00:09:26.831 --> 00:09:27.970
&gt;&gt; In that timeframe.

00:09:27.971 --> 00:09:28.795
&gt;&gt; That's fabulous.

00:09:28.796 --> 00:09:31.870
&gt;&gt; The first, in the goldfish retina,

00:09:31.871 --> 00:09:33.505
they found a neuron which had

00:09:33.506 --> 00:09:37.510
very thick fiber coming in
and it was an inhibitor,

00:09:37.511 --> 00:09:39.145
whatever that neuron was doing,

00:09:39.146 --> 00:09:40.315
when this thing fired,

00:09:40.316 --> 00:09:41.905
it stopped that neuron cold.

00:09:41.906 --> 00:09:44.110
&gt;&gt; Very interesting. So until you

00:09:44.111 --> 00:09:46.480
experience in that lab
got you thinking

00:09:46.481 --> 00:09:48.520
more deeply about the
mathematics that might

00:09:48.521 --> 00:09:51.220
be important in brains and minds.

00:09:51.221 --> 00:09:52.660
So it's interesting to
think how you went from

00:09:52.661 --> 00:09:55.240
this interest which clearly
is now that I understand

00:09:55.241 --> 00:09:57.190
your background and you've presented

00:09:57.191 --> 00:10:00.200
yesterday at your
distinguished lecture,

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:04.450
how that frames
a number of things that

00:10:04.451 --> 00:10:07.810
you did including the work on
recursion and so on,

00:10:07.811 --> 00:10:11.665
that behind this was interested
in some questions about minds?

00:10:11.666 --> 00:10:13.660
&gt;&gt; Yes, I did think that

00:10:13.661 --> 00:10:17.770
recursive function theory
and logic were the kinds of

00:10:17.771 --> 00:10:20.290
mathematics I needed
in order to build

00:10:20.291 --> 00:10:24.715
this manual of how the brain
works in order to understand-

00:10:24.716 --> 00:10:26.110
&gt;&gt; I think I can just
describe a little bit about

00:10:26.111 --> 00:10:27.670
the work on recursion that you did

00:10:27.671 --> 00:10:33.560
maybe in a broad sense of what
the intent and the results there.

00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:36.130
&gt;&gt; I'll describe one thing.

00:10:36.131 --> 00:10:36.280
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:10:36.281 --> 00:10:40.825
&gt;&gt; That's interesting
which is not mine,

00:10:40.826 --> 00:10:44.515
it's this proof that a Turing machine

00:10:44.516 --> 00:10:48.610
to simple type of computer can
start out on a blank tape,

00:10:48.611 --> 00:10:52.285
can actually print out
its own description on the tape.

00:10:52.286 --> 00:10:56.830
It's called the recursion
theorem and it shows you,

00:10:56.831 --> 00:10:58.570
exactly the proof
shows you how to build

00:10:58.571 --> 00:11:01.900
a Turing machine so that it
prints out its own description,

00:11:01.901 --> 00:11:04.870
and this is part of machine
that can then self-reproduce,

00:11:04.871 --> 00:11:06.504
you first print out a description,

00:11:06.505 --> 00:11:08.290
and then you go to
a constructor which

00:11:08.291 --> 00:11:10.915
sees the description
and then builds it.

00:11:10.916 --> 00:11:14.575
&gt;&gt; What does that imply
about systems that could

00:11:14.576 --> 00:11:18.910
reflect about their own thinking
processes for the same logics?

00:11:18.911 --> 00:11:23.590
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So that sound very related,

00:11:23.591 --> 00:11:25.720
I mean for one thing it

00:11:25.721 --> 00:11:28.435
means that Turing machine can
print out its own description,

00:11:28.436 --> 00:11:29.785
and then modify it,

00:11:29.786 --> 00:11:32.335
and then follow
these new instructions,

00:11:32.336 --> 00:11:35.770
which it tells you

00:11:35.771 --> 00:11:38.200
why I got interested in
recursive function theory,

00:11:38.201 --> 00:11:39.760
I would not do it that way.

00:11:39.761 --> 00:11:43.285
&gt;&gt; I understand. Also speaking
about doing it that way.

00:11:43.286 --> 00:11:47.200
Let's talk a bit
about this pursuit of

00:11:47.201 --> 00:11:52.105
some formalism that could

00:11:52.106 --> 00:11:57.085
describe a model of conscious
experience or consciousness.

00:11:57.086 --> 00:12:00.010
Of course when you mentioned
that word you end up

00:12:00.011 --> 00:12:02.740
navigating a space where you passed

00:12:02.741 --> 00:12:05.080
philosophers and
people are doing with

00:12:05.081 --> 00:12:09.730
imprecise terms and not making
good progress this idea.

00:12:09.731 --> 00:12:10.600
&gt;&gt; Its crazy actually.

00:12:10.601 --> 00:12:11.770
&gt;&gt; It's crazy out there, right.

00:12:11.771 --> 00:12:14.365
&gt;&gt; People think I'm actually nuts.

00:12:14.366 --> 00:12:16.539
What am I doing,

00:12:16.540 --> 00:12:18.460
so I have a friend Aegon Bala,

00:12:18.461 --> 00:12:19.840
she was 96 years old,

00:12:19.841 --> 00:12:21.940
still quite active and he

00:12:21.941 --> 00:12:25.810
absolutely refuses to believe
that a machine could actually

00:12:25.811 --> 00:12:29.140
feel the feelings he has when he

00:12:29.141 --> 00:12:32.740
fell in love or when he hears music.

00:12:32.741 --> 00:12:34.510
&gt;&gt; Doesn't he believe
that that will make

00:12:34.511 --> 00:12:37.240
incredible existence proofs
of homosapiens?

00:12:37.241 --> 00:12:38.785
&gt;&gt; Yes. So it's interesting.

00:12:38.786 --> 00:12:42.355
I have, yeah, how come?

00:12:42.356 --> 00:12:48.085
You know how human beings
think they are closest to God,

00:12:48.086 --> 00:12:49.540
that they are in the center of

00:12:49.541 --> 00:12:52.555
the universe and I've
really always hated that,

00:12:52.556 --> 00:12:54.880
I terribly hated that and I was so

